<p class="lettrine">
    Imagine a chant more than a thousand years old, handed down first by word of mouth, then
    written with little signs traced by hand&nbsp;: the <strong>neumes</strong>. These signs
    look nothing like our present-day notes&nbsp;; they are drawings that suggest the
    <em>gesture</em> of the voice — rising, falling, lingering, bouncing. Learning to read
    them is rather like learning to decipher an ancient script&nbsp;: strange at first, then
    surprisingly logical.
</p>

<h2>What is this about&nbsp;?</h2>

<h3>Gregorian chant, in a nutshell</h3>
<p>
    Gregorian chant is the official chant of the Roman Catholic liturgy, fixed between the
    <abbr title="8th and 9th centuries">8<sup>th</sup> and the 9<sup>th</sup>
    century</abbr>. It is sung <strong>in Latin</strong>, <strong>in a single voice</strong>
    (we call it <em>monodic</em>&nbsp;: everyone sings the same melody) and <strong>without
    any instrumental accompaniment</strong>. It is a «&nbsp;bare&nbsp;» chant, entirely at
    the service of the sacred text.
cré.
</p>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Definition — Neume</span>
    <p>
        A <strong>neume</strong> is a sign noting one or more sounds sung on a single
        syllable. The word comes from the Greek <span class="mot-latin">pneuma</span>,
        «&nbsp;breath&nbsp;». A neume may be worth a single note… or a whole little spray of
        notes thrown out on one vowel.
    </p>
</div>

<h3>Why a «&nbsp;triple&nbsp;» book&nbsp;?</h3>
<p>
    The <strong><em>Graduale Triplex</em></strong> (published by the abbey of Solesmes in
    1979) is a chant book that superimposes <strong>three scripts</strong> for one and the
    same chant — hence its name «&nbsp;triplex&nbsp;», triple&nbsp;:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
in the centre, modern <strong>square notation</strong>, on a staff, giving the exact pitch
of every note&nbsp;;
</li>
    <li>above, the <strong>neumes of Laon</strong>, copied from a manuscript of the 9<sup>th</sup> century&nbsp;;</li>
    <li>below, the <strong>neumes of St Gall</strong>, taken from other early manuscripts.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Looking at all three at once, you get both <em>the right note</em> (thanks to the square
    notation) and <em>the way to sing it</em> — lively, weighty, light, drawn out — thanks
    to the two early scripts. We shall go into all of this on <a href="{{
    path('paleo_en_triplex') }}">the page devoted to it</a>.
</p>

<div class="encadre astuce">
